                  <p><mark class="hl-def" data-pair="1">Cysts are usually asymptomatic unless they are secondarily infected, and they are usually detected accidentally on routine radiographs. The overlying mucosa is normal in color and consistency, and no sensory deficits from encroachment on nerves are found.</mark></p>
                  <p><mark class="hl-warn" data-pair="2">If the cyst has not expanded or thinned the cortical plate, normal contour and firmness are noted, when there is expansion of the cyst it leads to facial asymmetry and if the cyst has eroded through the cortical plate, fluctuation may be noted on palpation. Cysts can cause displacement of teeth or resorption of roots.</mark></p>
                  <p><mark class="hl-exam" data-pair="3">The epithelial lining of cysts on rare occasions undergoes ameloblastic or malignant changes. Therefore, all excised cystic tissue must be submitted for pathologic examination. Some cysts (e.g., odontogenic keratocysts) behave more aggressively in destructive characteristics and recurrence rates.</mark></p>
