            segments: [
              {
                enHtml: `<h4>SUBLINGUAL DERMOID, THYROGLOSSAL, BRANCHIAL &amp; FOREGUT CYSTS</h4>
                  <p><strong>SUBLINGUAL DERMOID CYST:</strong> <mark class="hl-def" data-pair="1">These are cysts above the hyoid and mylohyoid, immediately beneath the tongue, usually in the midline, occasionally to one side. They are lined by a keratinising stratified squamous epithelium-like skin, complete with associated sebaceous glands, sweat glands and sometimes hair follicles. Those without skin adnexae are called epidermoid cysts. A sublingual dermoid is more deeply placed than a ranula, lacks the bluish appearance and is firmer. They are asymptomatic when small, enlarge to interfere with speech or eating. Most present in the second or third decade. These cysts are removed by excision.</mark></p>
                  <p><strong>THYROGLOSSAL DUCT CYST:</strong> <mark class="hl-exam" data-pair="2">Develop from embryological epithelial remnants of the thyroglossal duct, anywhere along its path from the dorsum of tongue to the site of the thyroid gland. It is the commonest neck cysts. Classically the cyst rises on swallowing while the tongue moves upward. These cysts are removed surgically with the body of the hyoid bone and tissue along the line of the tract down to the gland, to ensure that all remnants and any ectopic gland are removed. This prevents recurrence and removal of all ectopic microscopic thyroid tissue, which can rarely be the site of development of a thyroid cancer.</mark></p>
                  <p><strong>BRANCHIAL CYST:</strong> <mark class="hl-class" data-pair="3">The five pharyngeal arches develop between weeks 2–6 in utero and give rise to many structures in the head and neck. Failure of fusion of the arches can leave embryological remnants in the neck that can give rise to branchial cysts. The commonest is the second branchial arch cyst, at the anterior border of sternomastoid muscle, just below the angle of the mandible. Sometimes the cysts open to the skin and are then known as branchial clefts. Branchial cysts can attain a large size and present in adolescents or adults up to age of 40.</mark></p>
